# CLAUDE.md — Next.js + SQLite SaaS Template

## Project Overview
This is a production Next.js SaaS application using SQLite (via Turso/libsql) for persistence.

## Tech Stack
- **Framework**: Next.js 14+ (App Router)
- **Database**: SQLite via Turso (libsql), Drizzle ORM
- **Auth**: NextAuth.js v5 with GitHub/Google OAuth
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui
- **Payments**: Stripe (subscriptions + one-time)
- **Deployment**: Vercel + Turso hosted DB
- **Testing**: Vitest + Playwright

## Architecture
```
src/
  app/           # Next.js App Router pages + API routes
    (auth)/      # Auth pages (login, register)
    (dashboard)/ # Protected SaaS dashboard
    api/         # API routes
  components/    # React components (shadcn/ui based)
    ui/          # Base UI components
    features/    # Feature-specific components
  lib/           # Shared utilities
    db/          # Drizzle schema, queries, migrations
    auth/        # NextAuth config + helpers
    stripe/      # Stripe helpers + webhook handlers
  hooks/         # Custom React hooks
  types/         # TypeScript types
```

## Database
- **ORM**: Drizzle with `drizzle-kit` for migrations
- **Schema**: `src/lib/db/schema.ts` — single source of truth
- **Migrations**: `drizzle/` folder, run with `pnpm db:migrate`
- **Queries**: Colocated in `src/lib/db/queries/` by domain
- **Connection**: Turso client in `src/lib/db/index.ts`

### Key tables
- `users` — auth + profile
- `subscriptions` — Stripe subscription state
- `teams` — multi-tenant support
- `team_members` — user-team relationships

## Auth
- NextAuth.js v5 with `@auth/drizzle-adapter`
- Providers: GitHub, Google (add in `src/lib/auth/config.ts`)
- Session strategy: JWT (stateless, edge-compatible)
- Protected routes: middleware at `src/middleware.ts`
- Role-based access: `user`, `admin`, `owner` on team_members

## API Conventions
- API routes in `src/app/api/`
- Use `NextResponse.json()` for responses
- Validate input with `zod` schemas
- Auth check: `const session = await auth()` at top of handler
- Error format: `{ error: string, code?: string }`
- Rate limiting: middleware-based per IP

## Stripe Integration
- Webhook handler: `src/app/api/webhooks/stripe/route.ts`
- Subscription sync: webhook events update `subscriptions` table
- Client helpers: `src/lib/stripe/client.ts`
- Price IDs: defined in `src/lib/stripe/plans.ts`
- Always verify webhook signatures

## Commands
```bash
pnpm dev          # Start dev server (port 3000)
pnpm build        # Production build
pnpm db:generate  # Generate migration from schema changes
pnpm db:migrate   # Apply migrations
pnpm db:studio    # Open Drizzle Studio (DB browser)
pnpm test         # Run Vitest unit tests
pnpm test:e2e     # Run Playwright E2E tests
pnpm lint         # ESLint + Prettier check
```

## Environment Variables
```env
DATABASE_URL=libsql://your-db.turso.io
DATABASE_AUTH_TOKEN=your-turso-token
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=your-secret
NEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
GITHUB_ID=oauth-client-id
GITHUB_SECRET=oauth-client-secret
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_...
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_...
```

## Code Style
- TypeScript strict mode — no `any` types
- Prefer server components; use `"use client"` only when needed
- Colocate related files (component + hook + types)
- Named exports only (no default exports except pages)
- Error boundaries at layout level
- Loading states via `loading.tsx` files
- Zod for all external input validation

## Testing
- **Unit**: Vitest for utils, hooks, API handlers
- **E2E**: Playwright for critical user flows
- **DB tests**: Use in-memory SQLite (`better-sqlite3`)
- Test files: `*.test.ts` next to source files
- E2E files: `tests/e2e/*.spec.ts`

## Deployment
- Push to `main` triggers Vercel deploy
- Environment variables set in Vercel dashboard
- Turso DB auto-replicates to edge locations
- Preview deployments on PR branches

## Do NOT
- Use `any` type — use `unknown` + type guards
- Store secrets in code — use environment variables
- Skip input validation on API routes
- Mutate state directly — use React state/actions
- Write raw SQL — use Drizzle query builder
- Deploy without running `pnpm build` locally first
